<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">War of the Austrian Succession</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1740">1740</a>–<a href="/years/1748">1748</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The War of the Austrian Succession was a European war fought from 1740 to 1748 over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a>'s right to inherit the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> lands after the death of Emperor Charles VI. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-great">Frederick the Great</a> seized most of Silesia, and the Bavarian elector Charles Albert was elected Emperor Charles VII in 1742. The Treaty of Aix-la-Chapelle in 1748 confirmed Maria Theresa's succession but left Silesia to Prussia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/war-of-the-austrian-succession.jpg" alt="War of the Austrian Succession" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: DavidDijkgraaf · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:War_of_The_Austrian_Succesion_Collage_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Mutual Pact of Succession of 1703 provided that if the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> died out in the male line, their possessions would pass first to the daughters of Emperor Joseph I and then to those of his younger brother Charles. Since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salic-law">Salic law</a> excluded women, the agreement needed the approval of the Habsburg territories and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-diet">Imperial Diet</a>. Emperor Charles VI died on 20 October 1740.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii">Frederick II</a> had become king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> on 31 May 1740, aged 28, and wished to overcome the limits that his scattered territories set on Prussian power. The death gave him a chance to take resource-rich Silesia before Augustus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> could act. Prussia's army of 80,000 was better trained than the Austrian one, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> had agreed to attack from the west.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> occupied Silesia in December 1740. At Mollwitz on 10 April 1741 Frederick made mistakes and left the field, and his deputy von Schwerin won a narrow victory. A Franco-Bavarian force marched along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> and took Linz on 14 September 1741, and with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> force of 20,000 advanced on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>. On 12 February 1742 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-vii-holy-roman-emperor">Charles Albert of Bavaria</a> was crowned Emperor Charles VII, the first non-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> emperor in 300 years, but Austrian troops captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> that day. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a> ended a secret truce with Frederick by publishing its terms, damaging his bond with his allies.</p><p>In 1743 the allied Pragmatic Army beat French troops at Dettingen, the last time a British monarch personally led troops in battle. After Charles VII died on 20 January 1745, his son Maximilian III Joseph signed the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fussen">Füssen</a> on 22 April, agreeing to vote for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a>'s husband Francis Stephen as emperor. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a> the French took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tournai">Tournai</a> after Fontenoy, then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghent">Ghent</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruges">Bruges</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a>, and won at Rocoux in October 1746. Elsewhere <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> fought at sea, in the war's North American theatre King George's War, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, where the French took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a> in September 1746.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>By early 1748 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> was close to bankruptcy, and a British naval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> had collapsed its customs receipts and caused food shortages. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xv">Louis XV</a> agreed to return the Austrian Netherlands, a decision that few of his countrymen understood and that gave rise to the phrase as stupid as the Peace. The Treaty of Aix-la-Chapelle, signed in October, confirmed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a> in her titles, but many signatories were unhappy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>'s insistence that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> cede Silesia undermined the Anglo-Austrian Alliance, since Austria felt it had gained little from large British subsidies. This led to the Diplomatic Revolution and, in 1756, the outbreak of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “War of the Austrian Succession” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The monarchy then lost Silesia to Frederick II of Prussia in the <b>War of the Austrian Succession</b>, failed to recover it in the Seven Years’ War despite victory at Kolín in 1757, and suffered repeated defeats by revolutionary France after 1792.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-monarchy">Habsburg Monarchy · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the <b>War of the Austrian Succession</b> Austrian troops occupied Genoa in 1746 until a popular rising, traditionally begun by the boy known as Balilla, drove them out.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-genoa">Republic of Genoa · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Fearing that Austria would retake Silesia, Prussia declared its intervention in the <b>War of the Austrian Succession</b> on behalf of Emperor Charles VII.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-7">7 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In the <b>War of the Austrian Succession</b>, an allied army under George II beat the French in Bavaria.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-27">27 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Second Silesian War, part of the <b>War of the Austrian Succession</b>, Frederick the Great's Prussian army decisively defeated an Austro-Saxon army under Prince Charles Alexander of Lorraine.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-4">4 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In the <b>War of the Austrian Succession</b>, Austrian forces defeated the French and then plundered Vilshofen.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-28">28 March in history</a></li></ul></section>
